1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,696
Your phone storage is killing people. I found out the hard way when my ex vanished completely.

2
00:00:06,996 --> 00:00:16,236
It started small. I cleared out old vacation screenshots, and the beaches in my physical photo albums turned to blank white paper.

3
00:00:16,536 --> 00:00:23,496
Then I deleted my high school group chat. Three childhood friends stopped texting, and their desks at work were empty.

4
00:00:23,796 --> 00:00:30,372
The cloud does not just store data. It anchors physical existence to our pocket devices.

5
00:00:30,672 --> 00:00:38,568
Panic set in. My thumb hovered over the trash icon containing my entire childhood video archive.

6
00:00:38,868 --> 00:00:46,596
If I delete the files, do the memories evaporate, or do the people holding them simply cease to breathe?

7
00:00:46,896 --> 00:00:55,560
I had to test the terrifying theory. I tapped empty on the folder labeled family vacation two thousand ten.

8
00:00:55,860 --> 00:01:02,172
My mother's portrait on the wall dissolved into grey dust before my eyes as the storage meter dropped.

9
00:01:02,472 --> 00:01:08,880
Now my phone battery is at one percent, and my hands are already turning completely transparent.

10
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:15,444
The screen goes black. Who will delete you when you finally fade away into nothingness?
